-- Computer Programming | ESE | medium | TestId 4900093511040129 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4900093511040129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical task in Computer Programming involves this requirement: describe a finite and unambiguous series of steps to transform defined input into output. Explain how Problem solving using flowchart and algorithm applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Computer Programming involves this requirement: identify the specific purpose or behaviour of History of C in Fundamentals of C. Explain how History of C applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Computer Programming involves this requirement: select a program branch based on a Boolean condition. Explain how Decision making statements applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Computer Programming involves this requirement: store and dereference a memory address to access a target object. Explain how Types of arrays applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** An algorithm is a finite sequence of unambiguous steps transforming input to output. It should terminate, be correct and use reasonable time and space.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Flowchart- Importance of flowchart; Symbols of flowchart; how to prepare flowchart; Algorithm design. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Computer Programming problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** History of C is covered in “Fundamentals of C”. A good answer should define its purpose, explain its main components or steps, connect it to Structure of C program, compilation and execution, Compiler and Interpreter, C tokens, provide a practical example and state how the result can be checked.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Structure of C program; compilation and execution; Compiler and Interpreter; C tokens. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Computer Programming problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** Conditional statements select a path based on a condition. Conditions should cover intended cases and be ordered to avoid unreachable branches.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as simple If statement; If-else; nested If- else; else-if ladder. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Computer Programming problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** A pointer stores a memory address. Dereferencing accesses the target; pointers should be initialized and used only while valid and correctly typed.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as declaration & initialization of 1-D array; operation in array; String- declaration & initialization; string functions. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Computer Programming problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Digital Logic and Design | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 490093200104015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (490093200104015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Logic and Design involves the syllabus topic digital and analog systems as applied within Introduction: 1. Explain how Digital and Analog systems applies, outline the steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Logic and Design involves the syllabus topic number system: binary number system (addition as applied within Number systems and binary codes : 6. Explain how Number System: Binary number system (addition applies, outline the steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Logic and Design involves the syllabus topic logic gates: positive and negative logic as applied within Logic Gates & Boolean Algebra 7. Explain how Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic applies, outline the steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Digital Logic and Design involves the syllabus topic combinational circuits and its implementations as applied within Combinational logic design: 7.5. Explain how Combinational Circuits and its implementations applies, outline the steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Digital and Analog systems appears in “Introduction: 1”. Define its role and features, relate it to logic level and pulse waveforms, elements of digital logic, Functions of digital logic, Introduction: 1, provide a practical example and state one process/design check or limitation. Confirm the details against official teaching material.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to logic level and pulse waveforms, elements of digital logic, Functions of digital logic, Introduction: 1. Include the relevant conditions, components, operating variables or formulas.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Logic and Design task and describe the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check against the stated objective and mention a limitation, error source or safety/design trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles/steps 3; application 2; verification 1. Confirm against official teaching material before use."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Number System: Binary number system (addition appears in “Number systems and binary codes : 6”. Define its role and features, relate it to subtraction, multiplication, Division), Octal and Hexadecimal Number system, provide a practical example and state one process/design check or limitation. Confirm the details against official teaching material.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to subtraction, multiplication, Division), Octal and Hexadecimal Number system, Conversion from binary to decimal. Include the relevant conditions, components, operating variables or formulas.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Logic and Design task and describe the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check against the stated objective and mention a limitation, error source or safety/design trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles/steps 3; application 2; verification 1. Confirm against official teaching material before use."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic appears in “Logic Gates & Boolean Algebra 7”. Define its role and features, relate it to NOT, NAND, NOR, X-OR Gate, provide a practical example and state one process/design check or limitation. Confirm the details against official teaching material.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to NOT, NAND, NOR, X-OR Gate, X-NOR Gate ( all gates with symbol. Include the relevant conditions, components, operating variables or formulas.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Logic and Design task and describe the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check against the stated objective and mention a limitation, error source or safety/design trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles/steps 3; application 2; verification 1. Confirm against official teaching material before use."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Combinational Circuits and its implementations appears in “Combinational logic design: 7.5”. Define its role and features, relate it to Arithmetic Circuits - Adders and Subtractor, BCD Adder, Look-Ahead Carry Generator, Multiplier, provide a practical example and state one process/design check or limitation. Confirm the details against official teaching material.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to Arithmetic Circuits - Adders and Subtractor, BCD Adder, Look-Ahead Carry Generator, Multiplier, Magnitude comparator. Multiplexer and it’s application. Include the relevant conditions, components, operating variables or formulas.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Logic and Design task and describe the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check against the stated objective and mention a limitation, error source or safety/design trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles/steps 3; application 2; verification 1. Confirm against official teaching material before use."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

